{"generated_at":"2026-10-10T13:10:59+00:00","window":{"start":"2011-01-03","end":"2026-10-09","requested_start":"2011-01-03","trading_days":3966,"n_returns":3965,"years":15.734127},"estimation":{"expected_returns":"compounded mean of daily simple returns, annualised at 252","covariance":"Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage toward constant variance, annualised at 252","shrinkage_delta":0.005792,"n_returns":3965},"rf_default":0.0406,"rf_source":"^IRX close 2026-10-09","universe":{"key":"etf_global","name":"Global cross-asset ETFs","file":"etf_global.toml","description":"Why ETFs rather than single stocks.\n\nMarkowitz needs a covariance matrix, and a covariance matrix estimated from a sample is the\nweakest part of the whole construction: with N assets it has N(N+1)/2 free parameters estimated\nfrom N*T numbers, so at N=120 and T=3,800 daily bars the ratio of observations to parameters is\nabout 63 \u2014 which sounds comfortable and is not, because the errors land in the smallest\neigenvalues, and the optimiser's job is precisely to lever the smallest eigenvalues. That is the\n\"error maximisation\" property: mean-variance optimisation is an estimation-error amplifier.\n\n120 mega-cap US stocks correlate at 0.4-0.6 with each other and 0.7+ with the index; their\ncovariance matrix is near-singular in the directions that matter, and the frontier that comes out\nis a thin sliver with 6-8 nonzero weights. That is a real and instructive result \u2014 it is the\nerror-maximisation demo \u2014 but it is a demo about estimation failure, not about diversification. A\ncross-asset ETF universe has genuinely different return generators (nominal bonds, TIPS, gold,\ncurrencies, energy) whose pairwise correlations run negative to mildly positive, so the frontier\nactually bends and the tangency portfolio holds something you would recognise as a portfolio."},"benchmark":"SPY","groups":["equity","fixed_income","real_asset_fx"],"group_labels":{"equity":"Equity","fixed_income":"Fixed income","real_a